<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/intermediate/stocks-and-bonds">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Stocks and bonds are the two primitive financial claims: <strong>ownership and
    debt</strong>. Everything in markets — funds, derivatives, entire economies of speculation —
    is built from these two. Understanding what you actually hold (a share of profits, or a loan
    contract) explains both the returns and the risks: the equity holder gets the residual after
    everyone is paid, which is why their upside is unlimited and their downside comes last; the
    bondholder gets a fixed stream first, which is why their upside is capped and their priority
    in trouble is near the front of the queue.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the two claims, their prices, and the index that holds them" widget="equationStep" config={sbCfg} />

  <h2>The stock: a residual claim on a business</h2>
  <p>
    A share of common stock is a slice of ownership with three attached rights: a claim on
    profits (paid as <strong>dividends</strong> or retained for growth), a vote on governance
    (electing the board), and a residual claim in liquidation — you are paid after employees,
    suppliers, lenders and bondholders, which is why equity is called the
    <strong>residual claim</strong>. That position explains the return profile: in good
    outcomes the residual is unbounded (a dollar of profit growth accrues entirely to
    shareholders, forever), in bad outcomes shareholders absorb the losses until wiped out.
    <strong>Limited liability</strong> caps that worst case at your investment — the legal
    invention that made modern equity markets possible, since investors will fund risky
    ventures only when failure cannot follow them home. Stocks first reach the market in an
    <strong>initial public offering</strong> (the company sells shares to the public and gets
    the money); afterwards they trade on <strong>secondary markets</strong> — exchanges like
    the NYSE, Nasdaq and LSE — where the company receives nothing and investors trade claims
    between themselves at prices set continuously by order books. Those prices are the
    market's running vote on the discounted value of all future profits.
  </p>

  <h2>The bond: a loan with a schedule</h2>
  <p>
    A bond is a standardised loan contract: the issuer borrows the <strong>face value</strong>
    (say 1,000), pays a <strong>coupon</strong> (say 5 per cent a year) and repays the face
    value at <strong>maturity</strong> (say 10 years). Issuers split into governments (US
    Treasuries, UK gilts, German Bunds — the "risk-free" curve in their own currency) and
    corporations, whose additional <strong>credit risk</strong> is graded by rating agencies
    from AAA down through investment grade to "high yield" — junk. The credit analysis
    looks at leverage, cash-flow coverage and collateral, and the market expresses the verdict
    as a <strong>spread</strong>: high-yield bonds might yield 3–6 percentage points more than
    Treasuries, compensation for default risk that occasionally materialises (2008, and the
    energy sector's 2015–16 defaults). One structural variant matters for history:
    <strong>securitisation</strong> — pooling thousands of loans (mortgages, car loans) into
    new bonds sold in slices. It spreads risk efficiently in principle; in 2008 the slices
    built from subprime mortgages proved far riskier than their ratings, and the mechanism
    transmitted a housing problem into a global banking crisis.
  </p>

  <h2>How prices are set: discounting, twice</h2>
  <p>
    Both claims are valued by the same idea — a future cash flow is worth less today —
    applied to different streams. For a bond, discount the coupons and principal at the
    <strong>yield to maturity</strong> the market demands. Worked example: the 1,000 bond,
    5 per cent coupon, 10 years. While market rates sit at 5 per cent it trades at par
    (1,000). If rates rise to 6 per cent, the same cash flows discounted at 6 per cent are
    worth about <strong>926</strong> — a 7.4 per cent price fall from a 1 per cent rate rise.
    That inverse price–yield relationship is mechanical, not a mood: your fixed 50 coupons
    simply compete with new issues paying more. Sensitivity to rates is summarised by
    <strong>duration</strong>: a 10-year bond loses roughly 7–8 per cent of price per 1
    percentage point rise; a 30-year bond, roughly 17–19 per cent — which is exactly what
    2022 demonstrated when aggressive rate rises produced the worst global bond year on
    record (major aggregate indices fell around 13–16 per cent, and UK gilts endured their
    worst year in decades).
  </p>
  <p>
    For a stock, the discounting runs over the company's entire future profit stream. The
    cleanest expression is the <strong>Gordon growth model</strong>: price = D<sub>1</sub>/(r − g),
    the next dividend divided by the gap between your required return and the growth rate.
    Example: a company paying a 4 dividend growing 3 per cent a year, with a required return
    of 9 per cent, is worth 4/(0.09 − 0.03) ≈ <strong>67</strong>. Notice the formula's
    sensitivity: the denominator is the difference of two hard-to-estimate numbers, which is
    why small changes in expected growth or rates move equity prices violently — and why
    practitioners fall back on multiples like the price-to-earnings ratio (the market
    typically pricing the S&amp;P 500 between roughly 15 and 25 times annual earnings) as a
    sanity check. When rates fall, both effects push prices up: future profits are discounted
    less. <strong>Rates are gravity for every asset</strong> — the single sentence that
    explains most of 2021's euphoria and 2022's crash.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "dividends are free money." On the ex-dividend date the
    share price falls by roughly the dividend paid — the company has moved cash from its own
    account to yours. Total return is what compounds; a dividend strategy is a preference
    for cash income, not an arbitrage.
  </p>

  <h2>Indices: the market in one number</h2>
  <p>
    An index tracks a basket: the S&amp;P 500 weights about 500 large US companies by market
    value and covers roughly 80 per cent of US equity capitalisation; the FTSE 100 does the
    same for the hundred largest London listings; the Nasdaq Composite tilts to technology.
    Indices are the benchmark that defines "beating the market" — and the benchmark that
    most professionals fail to beat: the SPIVA scorecards consistently find that around 90
    per cent of US large-cap active funds underperform the S&amp;P 500 over 15-year periods.
    The reason is structural, not stupidity: in a market where prices digest an enormous
    amount of analysis, the average invested dollar must earn the market return minus costs —
    and active management costs 1–2 per cent a year while index funds cost a few hundredths.
    The index <em>is</em> the average, kept cheap.
  </p>

  <h2>Credit analysis: how a bond's risk gets graded</h2>
  <p>
    What makes one corporate bond safe and another junk? The analyst's core questions are
    about solvency under stress. <strong>Leverage</strong>: debt relative to earnings or
    assets — a company owing three times its annual operating profit can usually ride out a
    bad year; one owing eight times may not. <strong>Interest coverage</strong>: operating
    profit divided by the interest bill — cover of 10 times is comfortable; below 2 times
    leaves the dividend of error near zero. <strong>Liquidity</strong>: cash and undrawn
    credit lines against debts falling due in the next year. Ratings agencies compress this
    analysis into letters: AAA to BBB− is investment grade (most regulated funds may hold
    it); below that, high yield. The grade matters mechanically, not just descriptively:
    downgrades force index-tracking holders to sell, which can push prices down further —
    the <em>fallen angel</em> dynamic. The reward for bearing that risk is the
    <strong>spread</strong>: in normal times high-yield issuers pay maybe 3–4 points over
    Treasuries; in panics the spread can blow out past 10 points, which is precisely when
    default risk (and opportunity) peaks. Reading spreads is reading the market's live
    estimate of how much trouble the economy is in.
  </p>

  <h2>Security markets: how a trade actually happens</h2>
  <p>
    The price you see quoted is produced by an <strong>order book</strong>: standing buy
    orders (bids) and sell orders (asks) ranked by price. The best bid and best ask bracket
    the <strong>spread</strong> — often a few cents on liquid stocks — and market makers earn
    it for standing ready to trade, which is what <strong>liquidity</strong> means: the
    ability to convert a claim into cash, quickly, without moving the price. Liquidity is
    the market's invisible variable: the same share can be effortless to trade one week and
    gappy the next, and in crises spreads widen exactly when you most need to exit. It is
    also the reason index construction matters — adding a stock to a major index forces
    passive funds to buy it, changing its liquidity and often its price. Two practical
    corollaries for an investor: trade liquid, broad instruments (the giants of the index
    world are liquid by construction), and prefer limit orders to market orders when size
    is large — the spread is small, but it is being paid by someone.
  </p>

  <h2>The trade-off, quantified: why hold both</h2>
  <p>
    Long-run US data (the Ibbotson series, back to 1926) puts large-cap stocks at about
    <strong>10 per cent a year nominal</strong> (roughly 7 per cent after inflation) against
    about <strong>5 per cent</strong> for bonds (roughly 2 per cent real) and 3 per cent for
    cash. The gap is the <strong>equity risk premium</strong> — payment for enduring drawdowns
    that reach 50 per cent or more (1929–32, 2000–02, 2007–09, 2022's near-miss). But the two
    claims also <em>fail differently</em>: stocks suffer long, deep bear markets while
    high-quality government bonds usually rally in exactly those crises (flight to safety in
    2008, 2020) — which is what makes bonds the diversifier and the source of money you can
    spend without selling stocks at the bottom. 2022 was the exception that proved the rule:
    inflation forced rates up, and stocks and bonds fell together, a reminder that the
    hedge is a tendency, not a law. Allocation between the two — not stock-picking — is the
    decision that explains most of a portfolio's behaviour over a lifetime.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four expert nuances. First, the <strong>yield curve</strong> — the map of yields across
    maturities — normally slopes up (longer loans cost more); its <em>inversion</em> (short
    yields above long) has preceded every US recession of the past half-century, a
    widely-studied signal that works better than most forecasting models. Second,
    <strong>dividend policy is nearly irrelevant in theory</strong> (Modigliani–Miller, 1961):
    in perfect markets, paying a dividend or retaining it leaves shareholder wealth
    unchanged — which is why the modern dominance of share buybacks (returning cash by
    retiring shares) is a tax-and-signal story, not a valuation one. Third, the
    <strong>equity premium puzzle</strong> (Mehra and Prescott, 1985): the historical premium
    is too large to be explained by standard risk-aversion models — either investors
    honestly price catastrophe risk we underestimate, or stocks were historically
    under-owned for institutional reasons; either way the future premium is probably
    thinner than the past's. Fourth, the <strong>negative-yield era</strong>: by late 2020
    about 18 trillion dollars of bonds traded at negative yields — lenders paying for the
    privilege — a monetary-policy artefact that has since vanished, but a permanent
    demonstration that "guaranteed" features of markets are regime-dependent. Hold both
    claims, know why each pays, and respect the discount rate: that is the whole subject in
    one habit.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ibbotson SBBI historical data; Bogle, Common Sense on Mutual Funds; Malkiel, A Random Walk Down Wall Street; SPIVA scorecards (S&amp;P Dow Jones Indices); Mehra &amp; Prescott (1985).</p>
</TopicPage>
